IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mc97-nouns`
IMPORT `mc97-status`

-- MAGNA CARTA (1297), LAYER 1: CHAPTER 1, "Confirmation of Liberties."
-- Source: aep-Edw1cc1929-25-9-extract.md, the heading (line 28) and the text of chapter I (line 29).
-- Two sentences. The first confirms that the Church of England shall be free and shall have all her whole Rights and
-- Liberties inviolable. The second grants the Liberties under-written to all the Freemen of the Realm and their Heirs.
-- Nothing here says the chapter is, or ever was, in force: the extract says only that the site shows it without a repeal
-- annotation, and that is not a statement about in-force status.

§ `Chapter 1: Confirmation of Liberties`

§§ `The first sentence: the Church of England`

-- gloss (not the text's): the sentence says the grant is made "to God"; the text does not say what standing that gives the
-- Church. This encoding states what shall be true of the Church (free; her whole Rights and Liberties inviolable) and
-- leaves open who may enforce it. See the fork register, F-C1-GOD.
-- src:29 | We have granted to God, and by this our present Charter have confirmed, for Us and our Heirs for ever, that the Church of England shall be free,
@ref Magna Carta (1297), chapter 1, first sentence, first limb (src:29)
GIVEN body IS A Body
GIVETH A Coverage
`chapter 1: the body shall be free` body MEANS
    IF body EQUALS `the Church of England`
    THEN `covered by the text`
    -- not covered, silent: the sentence names the Church of England and no other body.
    ELSE `not covered by the text`

-- src:29 | and shall have all her whole Rights and Liberties inviolable.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), chapter 1, first sentence, second limb (src:29)
GIVEN body IS A Body
GIVETH A Coverage
`chapter 1: the body shall have all her whole Rights and Liberties inviolable` body MEANS
    IF body EQUALS `the Church of England`
    THEN `covered by the text`
    -- not covered, silent: the sentence names the Church of England and no other body.
    ELSE `not covered by the text`

-- src:29 | FIRST, We have granted to God,
@ref Magna Carta (1297), chapter 1, first sentence, to whom the grant is said to be made (src:29)
GIVETH A STRING
`chapter 1: to whom the first sentence says the grant is made` MEANS "God"

-- "shall be free" and "shall have ... inviolable" are in the passive: the text names no one who is to keep the Church free or her Rights
-- inviolable, and none is invented. The answer is NOTHING, whoever asks (forks F-C1-GOD, F-C1-FOR).
-- src:29 | that the Church of England shall be free, and shall have all her whole Rights and Liberties inviolable.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), chapter 1, first sentence, who is to keep the Church free (src:29)
GIVETH A MAYBE Party
`chapter 1: the party the text names to keep the Church free` MEANS NOTHING

§§ `Both sentences: the parties the grants are made for, and the Liberties held of`

-- "for Us and our Heirs for ever" in the first sentence, and "of Us and our Heirs for ever" in the second: the King and his
-- Heirs. A Man of the Realm is not named here. The text does not say whether "for Us and our Heirs" binds the King and his Heirs
-- to keep the grant or only says on whose behalf it is made; the rule records only that these words are said of these parties
-- and of no one else (fork F-C1-FOR).
-- src:29 | for Us and our Heirs for ever, that the Church of England shall be free,
-- src:29 | to have and to hold to them and their Heirs, of Us and our Heirs for ever.
@ref Magna Carta (1297), chapter 1, "for Us and our Heirs" and "of Us and our Heirs" (src:29)
GIVEN party IS A Party
GIVETH A Coverage
`chapter 1: the grants are made for, and the Liberties are held of, the party` party MEANS
    IF party EQUALS `the King` OR party EQUALS `an Heir of the King`
    THEN `covered by the text`
    -- not covered, silent: the text says "for Us and our Heirs" and "of Us and our Heirs" of these parties and nothing of any other (F-C1-FOR).
    ELSE `not covered by the text`

§§ `The second sentence: the Freemen of the Realm and their Heirs`

-- gloss (not the text's): "Freeman" and "Heir" are not defined by the text; the asker says whether they are one.
-- If the asker is both a Freeman and an Heir of a Freeman, the sentence is read as first reaching the Freeman.
-- src:29 | We have granted also, and given to all the Freemen of our Realm,
-- src:29 | these Liberties under-written, to have and to hold to them and their Heirs,
@ref Magna Carta (1297), chapter 1, second sentence (src:29)
GIVEN `a Freeman of the Realm` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an Heir of a Freeman of the Realm` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Holding under chapter 1`
`chapter 1: how the Liberties under-written are held` `a Freeman of the Realm` `an Heir of a Freeman of the Realm` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `a Freeman of the Realm` THEN `holds as a Freeman of the Realm`
        IF `an Heir of a Freeman of the Realm` THEN `holds as an Heir of a Freeman of the Realm`
        -- not covered, undefined term: "Freeman" and "Heir" are not defined, and the sentence says nothing of anyone who is neither.
        OTHERWISE `not covered by chapter 1's second sentence`

§§ `The Liberties under-written: which chapters this text shows`

-- gloss (not the text's): "these Liberties under-written" points at what follows chapter 1; the text does not list them.
-- This rule reports only which of the chapters 2 to 37 have words in this text (no repeal annotation shown), by asking
-- the status function in mc97-status.l4. It does not say what the Liberties under-written are. See F-C1-LIBERTIES.
--
-- Scope of the FALSE below: the extract's status table, and nothing else (fork F-NOTCOVERED). JUST FALSE is answered only where the
-- extract says in its own words that the chapter is repealed ("Each repealed chapter appears in <Body> as an empty provision").
-- Chapter 37 and a number outside the cc. 1-37 the extract names get NOTHING: see the comments on those branches.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`status table: words are shown for chapter` n MEANS
    CONSIDER `the status shown for chapter` n
        WHEN `no repeal annotation shown` THEN JUST TRUE
        -- The table says in its own words that the chapter is repealed, and the extract says a repealed chapter is an empty provision.
        WHEN `shown repealed` entry THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING, for the reason "points outside the text's own words": the table says "repealed in part" and does not say which
        -- words remain; the extract author's reading of the residue is F-SAVING-C37, which this answer does not take.
        WHEN `shown repealed in part` entry THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING, for the reason "an unclosed list": the editorial note says the chapter numbers "includes cc. 1-37", not "only".
        WHEN `outside the cc. 1-37 the extract names` THEN NOTHING

GIVEN lowest IS A NUMBER
      highest IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A LIST OF NUMBER
`the chapters with words shown from` lowest `up to` highest MEANS
    IF lowest GREATER THAN highest
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE
        CONSIDER `status table: words are shown for chapter` lowest
            WHEN JUST TRUE THEN lowest FOLLOWED BY `the chapters with words shown from` (lowest PLUS 1) `up to` highest
            OTHERWISE `the chapters with words shown from` (lowest PLUS 1) `up to` highest

-- src:29 | these Liberties under-written,
@ref Magna Carta (1297), chapter 1, "these Liberties under-written" (src:29)
GIVETH A LIST OF NUMBER
`chapter 1: the chapters after chapter 1 for which this text shows words` MEANS
    `the chapters with words shown from` 2 `up to` `the last chapter number`
